{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Fraction in Lowest Terms.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Fraction in Lowest Terms.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"fraction in lowest terms fraction in lowest terms","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Fraction in Lowest Terms.md","fileName":"Fraction in Lowest Terms.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Fraction in Lowest Terms","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-05 23:41\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[Fractions and Decimal Forms]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Fractions and Decimal Forms","Numerator","Denominator","Equivalent Fraction","algebraessentials.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Fraction in Lowest Terms.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Fraction in Lowest Terms.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"fraction in lowest terms fraction in lowest terms","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Fraction in Lowest Terms.md","fileName":"Fraction in Lowest Terms.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Fraction in Lowest Terms","heading":"Fraction in Lowest Terms","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"A fraction is in lowest terms when its [[Numerator]] and [[Denominator]] share no whole-number factor greater than one. It is the reduced representative of its family of [[Equivalent Fraction]]s with the smallest positive denominator.\n\nReduction divides both components by common factors until none remain. For example,\n\n$$\\frac{104}{120}=\\frac{26}{30}=\\frac{13}{15}.$$\n\nDifferent valid cancellation sequences reach the same reduced form. This uniqueness depends on the fact that natural numbers have unique prime factorizations.","tags":["baby"],"links":["Fractions and Decimal Forms","Numerator","Denominator","Equivalent Fraction","algebraessentials.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6